lass="enc-snip">Irkutsk is a city in eastern Siberia, Russia, on the Angara river near Lake Baikal.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/irminsul"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/irminsul.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Irminsul</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Irminsul was a sacred pillar, perhaps a great tree trunk, venerated by the pagan Saxons of northern Germany.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/irnerius"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/irnerius.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Irnerius</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Irnerius was an Italian jurist who taught Roman law at Bologna in the early 12th century and is regarded as the founder of the Bologna school of glossators.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/iron-age"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/iron-age.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Iron Age</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">The Iron Age is the period in which iron replaced bronze as the main metal for tools and weapons, beginning in the eastern Mediterranean and Near East around 1200 BCE…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/iron-crown-of-lombardy"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/iron-crown-of-lombardy.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Iron Crown of Lombardy</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Iron Crown of Lombardy is a medieval crown of gold and jewels, kept in the cathedral of Monza, whose inner band was said to be made from a nail of the True Cross.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/iron-curtain"><span class="enc-name">Iron Curtain</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">The Iron Curtain was the political, military and physical boundary that divided Europe after the Second World War, separating the Soviet Union and the communist states…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/iron-gate-central-asia"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/iron-gate-central-asia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Iron Gate (Central Asia)</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Iron Gate is a narrow mountain pass in the Baysun range of southern Uzbekistan, on the route between Samarkand and the Amu Darya.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/iron-gates"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/iron-gates.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Iron Gates</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Iron Gates are the gorge of the Danube where it cuts through the Carpathian and Balkan mountains, today forming part of the border between Serbia and Romania.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/iron-gates-algeria"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/iron-gates-algeria.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Iron Gates (Algeria)</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Iron Gates of Algeria, known in French as the Portes de Fer, are a narrow pass through the Biban mountains between Algiers and Constantine.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/iron-plough"><span class="enc-name">Iron plough</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">The iron plough, or iron-tipped plough, is a plough whose cutting edge is made of iron, allowing deeper and more efficient tillage.</span></a></li></ul>
